      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Rupture of atheromatous plaques leading to acute coronary syndromes usually occur in lipid-reach and well-developed coronary lesions. We describe 2 unusual patients with acute coronary syndromes in whom there was angiographic and intravascular ultrasound evidence of an intraluminal thrombus overlying a small, nonocclusive plaque in an enlarged coronary artery."},
